1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:00,462
Part one.

2
00:00:00,888 --> 00:00:01,938
The night math changed.

3
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,025
It's a Tuesday evening in New York.

4
00:00:04,438 --> 00:00:07,012
Rutgers number theorist Alex Kontorovich is at a

5
00:00:07,025 --> 00:00:09,438
gala at the National Museum of Mathematics,

6
00:00:09,712 --> 00:00:11,812
a whiskey in one hand and his phone

7
00:00:11,825 --> 00:00:12,388
in the other.

8
00:00:12,812 --> 00:00:13,712
He keeps refreshing,

9
00:00:13,975 --> 00:00:16,475
because OpenAI is about to release something around

10
00:00:16,488 --> 00:00:17,175
six o'clock.

11
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:18,275
Then it drops.

12
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:20,262
Seven hundred solved problems.

13
00:00:20,675 --> 00:00:22,738
And the second thing he sees is quasi

14
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:23,463
R H,

15
00:00:23,850 --> 00:00:26,112
a partial version of the Riemann hypothesis,

16
00:00:26,388 --> 00:00:28,513
the most famous open problem in his field.

17
00:00:28,938 --> 00:00:29,650
His first thought:

18
00:00:29,962 --> 00:00:31,388
that can't be what's in this drop.

19
00:00:31,812 --> 00:00:33,725
He says he simply could not compute what

20
00:00:33,738 --> 00:00:34,400
he was seeing.

21
00:00:34,812 --> 00:00:37,788
At the same gala is MIT's Ankur Moitra.

22
00:00:38,225 --> 00:00:40,000
For him it wasn't a total surprise.

23
00:00:40,450 --> 00:00:42,050
For weeks there had been rumors that the

24
00:00:42,125 --> 00:00:44,375
biggest problems in his area had fallen,

25
00:00:44,775 --> 00:00:46,712
including the Unique Games Conjecture.

26
00:00:47,138 --> 00:00:49,512
One colleague even rushed out his own paper,

27
00:00:49,812 --> 00:00:51,525
with a footnote saying that because of the

28
00:00:51,538 --> 00:00:53,900
rumors, he couldn't wait any longer.

29
00:00:54,325 --> 00:00:55,700
Just hours before the drop,

30
00:00:56,112 --> 00:00:57,738
Ankur asked Subhash Khot,

31
00:00:58,025 --> 00:01:00,488
the man who proposed the Unique Games Conjecture,

32
00:01:00,788 --> 00:01:02,625
whether he would read an AI proof of

33
00:01:02,638 --> 00:01:02,812
it.

34
00:01:03,225 --> 00:01:04,238
Khot's answer was simply:

35
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:04,812
no.

36
00:01:05,238 --> 00:01:06,887
And this wasn't even the first shock.

37
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:08,250
A few weeks earlier,

38
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:10,612
Alex gave a talk at Princeton explaining that

39
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:13,975
AI was still years away from formalizing Fermat's

40
00:01:13,988 --> 00:01:15,875
Last Theorem in the proof checker Lean.

41
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:17,025
Two days later,

42
00:01:17,250 --> 00:01:19,038
Anthropic released exactly that.

43
00:01:19,512 --> 00:01:21,625
Thirteen million lines of verified code.

44
00:01:22,038 --> 00:01:23,587
As far as Alex understands,

45
00:01:23,838 --> 00:01:25,475
two or three people were involved,

46
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:27,362
and most of their input was typing:

47
00:01:27,850 --> 00:01:28,400
keep going.

48
00:01:28,812 --> 00:01:30,175
Then came Navier-Stokes.

49
00:01:30,612 --> 00:01:33,000
For Alex, the quasi Riemann night was round

50
00:01:33,012 --> 00:01:33,338
three.
